|~ `::error`, the kind every error is a value of, and what is declared
   under it: the refusal of an error whose path is no vector of stops,
   the facts a refusal may carry, and the words of errors. The error at
   rest, the value `!|` catches a failure to, is `::error{…}`, or the tag
   of the site that refused stacked over it, and its walk passes
   `::error` on its way to the map, so a word declared here reaches every
   error.
   The decisions it exists for: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:error
  |~~ An error is the facts of a failure as a value: what refused, on
      what, and the path the failure took, under the tag of the site
      that refused, which names the page that tells how to repair the
      call. A step that fails answers an error raised, a failure; a
      reader learning a tool fails often, and the failure brings what
      the reader needs at the moment it is cheapest to give.

      An error at rest is written with a bang, `!{:reason :late}`, the
      tag of its site before the bang, `::Late!{:by 3}`; it is
      `::error{…}` under that tag, and prints with its bang, as a set
      prints with its hash. It is a value like any other: a container
      holds it, its facts read as fields, `!{:reason :late} | get /reason`
      answering `:late`, and JSON writes it as a value under its tag.

      Raised by `raise`, an error is a failure, which lives in the pipe
      alone; a host prints it as its error at rest under the line
      `|~| raised, and no !| caught it`, which a reading passes over, so
      the print reads back as the error at rest, as `!|` grounds it.
      `|` passes a failure by, writing its path, and `!|` alone catches
      it, its step running on the error at rest: `"x" | add 1 !| type`
      answers the site, `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`, and
      `trail` the path. No value holds a failure, so a literal, a name
      or a fact whose part fails answers the failure.
      ::snippet~(::error | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::explanation | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps |~~ The facts of the failure, a map. ~~| ::map
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ An error at rest holds the facts of a failure as a map,
            `!{:reason :late}`; a payload of another kind is refused, and a
            value of another kind is held as a fact, `!{:value [1]}`.
        ~~|
    | throws :TrailNotVecError
        |~~ `:trail` among the facts of an error is its path, a vector of
            stops the runtime writes, `{:step :subject :skipped}`, from
            the step where it arose to the step where it is read. A
            `raise` of a map that writes a value that is no plain vector
            under `:trail`, a set, a quote and a doc among them, is
            refused as it makes the error. A `raise` that writes the
            `:trail` it read goes on with that path, and one that leaves
            it out starts a path at its own step.
            ::link~(::error | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :name
        |~~ The name a reader asked for, a keyword or a tag, where nothing
            answers it.
        ~~|
        #[::keyword ::tag]
    | carries :addresses
        |~~ The addresses where a word of that name lives, one for each
            kind that answers it.
        ~~|
        ::set
    | carries :nearest
        |~~ The names a step could have meant where it missed. For a name,
            a key or a tag nothing declares, those nearest in spelling:
            within a slip of the typing hand, an insertion, a deletion, a
            substitution or a swap of two neighbours, up to a third of its
            length and one at least, the nearest of them alone; beside
            them a name that begins it or that it begins, three letters at
            least, `multiply` for `mul`; and the words the page of a word
            it begins with links whose names end as it goes on, `sumOf`
            for `addOf`. For a word the kind of the subject lacks, the
            words the pages of the word lead to that the subject answers
            and that answer a kind the word answers, `print` and `json`
            for the `toString` of a vector. In the order of their
            spelling, and `[]` where none is near.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | carries :read
        |~~ The read the step was likely meant for, where a path stands in
            place of the value it names: the step again with the path read
            by `get`, `/tags | count` read as `get /tags | count` and
            `mul /qty` as `mul (get /qty)`.
        ~~|
        ::quote
    | carries :path
        |~~ The path a read followed where it missed, the key or the place
            it missed named beside it.
        ~~|
        ::path
    | carries :index
        |~~ The place of the element refused, counted from zero. ~~|
        ::number
    | carries :expected
        |~~ The kinds the holder takes, where a value of none of them was
            refused, `#[::number]`.
        ~~|
        #[::tag]
    | carries :actualType |~~ The kind of the value refused. ~~| ::tag
    | carries :places
        |~~ How many places the inputs of the word read, where the call
            gave more values.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | carries :actualValue
        |~~ The value refused, where it is not the subject that the first
            stop of the trail holds.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | carries :leftType |~~ The kind of the value on the left. ~~| ::tag
    | carries :rightType |~~ The kind of the value on the right. ~~| ::tag
    | carries :field
        |~~ The field that does not fit, null when the payload is no map.
        ~~|
        #[::keyword ::null]
    | carries :printed
        |~~ The text a value prints as, its literal: a step that reads
            back as another step or as none, or an answer the JSON channel
            has no form for.
        ~~|
        ::string
    | proves :writesAnErrorAtRestByItsBang
        |~~ The bang writes an error at rest of the kind of errors. ~~|
        ~(!{:detail "boom"} | type | eq ::error)
    | proves :standsOverItsKind
        |~~ Beneath the site's tag lies an `::error`. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| payload | type | eq ::error)
    | proves :readsItsFacts
        |~~ An error's facts read as fields. ~~|
        ~(::error{:k 1} | get /k | eq 1)
    | proves :takesAFactOnTheWay
        |~~ A fact added on the fail track rides on. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| union {:request "r-1"} | raise !| get /request | eq "r-1")
    | proves :recoversByItsType
        |~~ A failure of a known type recovers. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError) ~(0) ~(raise) | eq 0)
    | proves :raisesAgainOtherwise
        |~~ A failure of another type raises again as itself. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::Other) ~(0) ~(raise) !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector is no map of facts. ~~|
        ~(::error[1] !| type | eq ::error/PayloadKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :TrailNotVecError/refusesANumberForATrail
        |~~ A number given as a trail is refused. ~~|
        ~(!{:kind :oops :trail 5} | raise !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::error/TrailNotVecError ::number])
    | proves :TrailNotVecError/refusesATrailLaidOverAFailure
        |~~ A trail of a number laid over an error is refused when it is
            raised.
        ~~|
        ~(!{:kind :oops} | raise | count !| union {:trail 5} | raise !| type | eq ::error/TrailNotVecError)
    | proves :TrailNotVecError/startsATrailAtTheRaise
        |~~ An error raised without a trail starts its trail at the raise.
        ~~|
        ~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| minus #[:trail] | raise | add 1
          !| get /trail | map /step | eq [~(raise)])
    | proves :TrailNotVecError/refusesASetAQuoteAndADoc
        |~~ A set, a quote and a doc are no plain vector of stops. ~~|
        ~([#[1] ~(a) |~~ d ~~|] | map ~(get {:trail ~(it)} | raise !| type) | eq [::error/TrailNotVecError ::error/TrailNotVecError ::error/TrailNotVecError])
    | proves :read/namesAWrittenPathAlone
        |~~ A path a name holds is no path the step wrote, and names no
            read.
        ~~|
        ~(:p /qty | 5 | mul p !| query /read | eq null)

:error/trail
  |~~ Answers the path of the error, a vector of stops from the step
      that raised it to the `!|` that opened it, each a map of the
      `:step`, the `:subject` that step met, and the steps `:skipped`
      after it: `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail` answers the one stop of
      `add 1`, which met `"x"` and skipped `mul 2`. A step that ran the
      step that raised adds its stop after it, the `map` that ran an
      element among them, so the path reads outward:
      `[1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | map /step` answers
      `[~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))]`. An error at rest that flew no step
      holds no `:trail` and answers `[]`, `::error{:a 1} | trail`. The
      subject is an error at rest, the value `!|` catches a failure to:
      under `|` a failure passes `trail` by, as it passes every step, and
      a value that is no error is refused, a map with the key `:trail`
      among them.
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc) ::link~(::error | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The stops of the path the error took. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotErrorError
        |~~ `trail` reads the path of an error at rest, the value `!|`
            catches a failure to, `"x" | add 1 !| trail`; the key `:trail`
            of a plain map is read by `/trail`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersNothingForAnErrorThatFlewNoStep
        |~~ An error that flew no step holds no path. ~~|
        ~(::error{:a 1} | trail | eq [])
    | proves :holdsTheStepThatRaised
        |~~ The trail of an error raised at once holds the one step. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trail | count | eq 1)
    | proves :meetsTheSubjectOfTheStep
        |~~ The stop holds the subject the step met. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trail | first | get /subject | eq "x")
    | proves :namesTheStepsSkipped
        |~~ The stop of the step that raised names the steps skipped after
            it.
        ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail | first | get /skipped | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :readsOutward
        |~~ The step that ran an element adds its stop after the
            element's.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | map /step | eq [~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))])
    | proves :SubjectNotErrorError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map with the key `:trail` is no error. ~~|
        ~({:trail []} | trail !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::error/trail/SubjectNotErrorError ::map])
    | impl ~(query /trail | coalesce [])

